  "question_type": "LA",
  "question": "The area common to the circle $x^2+y^2=4$ and the parabola $y^2=2x$ lies in the first quadrant. Find this area.",
  "answeroption": "A",
  "explanation": "Intersection from $x^2+2x=4$ gives $x=-1+\\sqrt{5}$.<br /><br />Area $=\\int_{0}^{-1+\\sqrt{5}}[\\sqrt{4-x^2}-\\sqrt{2x}]dx$. Evaluating using standard formulas yields the exact expression:<br /><br />$\\frac{-1+\\sqrt{5}}{2}\\sqrt{2(\\sqrt{5}-1)} + 2\\sin^{-1}\\left(\\frac{-1+\\sqrt{5}}{2}\\right) - \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}(-1+\\sqrt{5})^{3/2}$.",
  "explanation_image": [
    "https://d-p-s.s3.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com/maths_question_explanations/69a8453764fcf4cdd5b4bab1/1778592832-0.jpeg?t=1778592832957"
  ],
  "options": {
    "A": "$\\frac{-1+\\sqrt{5}}{2}\\sqrt{2(\\sqrt{5}-1)} + 2\\sin^{-1}\\left(\\frac{-1+\\sqrt{5}}{2}\\right) - \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}(-1+\\sqrt{5})^{3/2}$",
    "B": "$\\frac{1+\\sqrt{5}}{2}\\sqrt{2(\\sqrt{5}+1)} + 2\\sin^{-1}\\left(\\frac{1+\\sqrt{5}}{2}\\right)$",
    "C": "$2\\sin^{-1}\\left(\\frac{-1+\\sqrt{5}}{2}\\right)+\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}(-1+\\sqrt{5})^{3/2}$",
